@using System
@using Sandbox
@using Sandbox.UI
@namespace Skafinity
@inherits PanelComponent
@implements Component.DontExecuteOnServer

@*
	TEMPORARY — a test card, not part of the library's UI. Delete it (and skafinity_slider_test)
	once the sliders are settled.

	It exists to answer one question in isolation: does a stock SliderControl draw AT ALL in this
	project? The music board is not a fair place to ask, because it has a stylesheet, a runtime
	palette, inline styles and five columns of layout around every slider. Here there is a card, a
	label and three sliders, and nothing else.

	NOTHING styles the sliders. The rules at the foot of this file dress the CARD — it has to be
	visible and roughly middle-of-screen to be looked at — and stop there, except for the one
	labelled copy of gambit's `.wslider`, which is the whole point of the comparison:

	  1. bare        — no class, no styles, no attributes but the value ones.
	  2. .wslider    — width/height/pointer-events on the control, exactly what gambit and
	                   terryball do, and the only thing they do.
	  3. boxed       — the same, wrapped in a div with a visible background, so an invisible
	                   slider can still be told apart from a slider that is not there.

	Whichever of these draws is the shape the board should use. If none does, the stock control
	cannot be drawn in this project at all and the board needs its own — and the two probes at the
	foot of the card say whether that is the slider's problem or the whole base stylesheet's.

	The variable being chased is not markup. Test 2 is already gambit's usage verbatim, and both
	siblings are `@inherits PanelComponent` with no package references, exactly like this. What
	differs is that they are `"Type": "game"` and this library is `"Type": "library"` — so the
	question is whether a library's UI gets the base addon's stylesheets at all.
*@

<root>
	<div class="card">
		<div class="line">skafinity slider test — value @Value.ToString( "0" )</div>

		<div class="line">1. bare (no class, no styles)</div>
		<SliderControl Min="@(0f)" Max="@(100f)" Step="@(1f)" Value="@Value"
					   OnValueChanged="@( (float v) => Set( v ) )"></SliderControl>

		<div class="line">2. .wslider (gambit / terryball's shape)</div>
		<SliderControl class="wslider" Min="@(0f)" Max="@(100f)" Step="@(1f)" Value="@Value"
					   OnValueChanged="@( (float v) => Set( v ) )"></SliderControl>

		<div class="line">3. .wslider inside a visible box</div>
		<div class="box">
			<SliderControl class="wslider" Min="@(0f)" Max="@(100f)" Step="@(1f)" Value="@Value"
						   OnValueChanged="@( (float v) => Set( v ) )"></SliderControl>
		</div>

		<div class="line">drag any of them — the number above follows whichever one works</div>

		@* ── The other half of the question ──────────────────────────────────────────────
		   Not sliders: probes for whether a BASE-ADDON stylesheet reaches this project at all.
		   Both are stock and neither is touched by anything here.

		   4 is a SwitchControl — another razor component in the base addon with a stylesheet of
		   its own, and one that draws something unmistakable: a 48x16 pill with a round grey
		   knob in it. If that pill is there, base stylesheets DO apply and the slider's trouble
		   is its own. If it is as invisible as the sliders, then no base stylesheet is reaching
		   this project, and no amount of markup will draw a control whose every part is sized by
		   one — which is the difference between here and the games this shape was copied from.

		   5 is a stock <button>, whose chrome is a plain class rule (.button in Button.cs.scss)
		   rather than a component's own sheet. It separates "no base stylesheets at all" from
		   "no base COMPONENT stylesheets". *@
		<div class="line">4. stock SwitchControl — a base component with its own stylesheet</div>
		<SwitchControl></SwitchControl>

		<div class="line">5. stock button — base chrome from a plain class rule</div>
		<button class="button">stock button</button>
	</div>
</root>

<style>
	/* The CARD only. Nothing below touches a slider except .wslider, which is quoted from gambit. */
	SkafinitySliderTest {
		position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
		justify-content: center; align-items: center;
		pointer-events: none;
		font-family: Consolas, monospace;

		.card {
			flex-direction: column;
			width: 600px;
			padding: 20px;
			gap: 10px;
			background-color: #101010;
			border: 2px solid #ffffff;
			pointer-events: all;
		}

		.line { font-size: 16px; color: #ffffff; }

		/* A visible floor under slider 3, so "invisible" and "absent" can be told apart. */
		.box { background-color: #303030; padding: 4px; }

		/* gambit's, verbatim. */
		.wslider {
			width: 100%;
			height: 24px;
			pointer-events: all;
		}
	}
</style>

@code
{
	/// <summary>Shared by all three, so whichever one accepts a drag moves the number at the top.</summary>
	public float Value { get; set; } = 50f;

	void Set( float v ) { Value = v; }

	protected override int BuildHash() => HashCode.Combine( Value );
}
